## 1. Introduction
The ever-increasing demand of miniaturization and portable electronic devices have unprecedentedly motivated scientists to develop the micro-supercapacitors with the merits of small volume, high volumetric energy, long cycling stability, fast charge/discharge, etc. [1–3]. However, the volumetric energy density of the current micro-devices is much lower than expected [4]. Normally, the feasible method to realize high volumetric energy density is enhancing the packing density of electrode materials and combining with the high capacity electrode materials [5–7]. When preparation of the traditional powder electrode, the conductive agent, binder and metal collector are necessary, which always lead to a complex process, a serious reduction of volumetric capacity and severely limiting its application in high volumetric energy density supercapacitors [8,9]. Therefore, it is an efficient measurement to develop the self-supporting metal-free integrated electrodes and obtain the high volumetric energy density supercapacitors.
Among the typical two-dimensional (2D) nanomaterials structure, graphene is a promising candidate in supercapacitor energy storage when used as a self-supporting electrode material [10]. In particular, it has a large specific surface area, excellent conductivity, which is conducive to ion diffusion and electronic transmission, resulting in high energy density, excellent rate performance as well as good cycle stability [11,12]. However, the low packing density of graphene-based electrode $(<0.5\ \mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3},\$ leads to a low volumetric energy density, which seriously restricts its application in lightweight portable devices [13]. Some studies showed that combine graphene with high capacitance pseudocapacitor compound, which not only improved the packing density of graphene, but also enhanced the overall capacitance [14,15]. Such as Niu et al. prepared a novel macroporous film utilizing $N\mathrm{.}$ -doped hollow carbon spheres and $\mathsf{C o}_{9}\mathsf{S}_{8}$ embedded in graphene film with the mass density of $1.56\ \mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ . When used as a self-support electrode, the hybrid structure showed a maximum volumetric capacitance of $842.4~\mathrm{~F~}~\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ [16]. Yan’s group fabricated a graphene nanomesh-carbon nanotube $/\mathsf{M o O}_{3-\mathbf{X}}$ nanocomposite with a density of $2.26\ g\ c m^{-3}$ and the sandwiched structure displayed a high volumetric capacity of $692\thinspace{\mathsf{C}}\thinspace{\mathsf{c m}}^{-3}$ [17].
The metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have a good application prospect in the field of high-performance supercapacitors due to their discretely ordered channel, large internal surface area and rich electrochemical active components [18–20]. In particular, the MOFs-based derivatives usually retain the original porous structure and have the structural characteristics of pore connectivity and surface hydrophilicity, which is advantageous to promote the transport and diffusion of ions and offer more active sites to obtain higher electrochemical performance [21–24]. Among various structures, the 2D MOFs show a short ion transport path and large open surface, exhibited the excellent rapid charge-discharge performances [25]. Recently, Yang et al. reported the cobalt sulfde quantum dots embedded within the interlayer of MOFs derived NiCoZn-LDH based on in-situ selective vulcanization of Co on carbon fibers. The hybrid material delivered an ultrahigh electrochemical capacitance of $2504\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ at $1\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}$ [26]. Guan et al. constructed the hollow and porous $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ nanosheet arrays on flexible carbon fibers by using the Co-ZIF sheets as precursors. This composite electrode showed a high capacitance of $1055.3\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at a current density of $2.5\operatorname{mA}\operatorname{cm}^{-2}$ [27]. To the best of our knowledge, there are lack of the report on the combination of MOFs derived ternary metal sulfde arrays and CNTs/graphene conductive substrate as electrode material with high volume capacity.
In this study, we demonstrated a novel strategy for designing and preparing a three-dimensional (3D) reduced graphene oxide $(\Gamma\mathsf{G O})/$ CNTs network conductive substrate supported MOFs-derived ZnCoNiS nanosheet arrays. The rGO/CNTs conductive substrate effectively enhanced the conductivity of the whole electrode, and its porous structure was conducive to the transport of electrolyte. The ZnCoNiS nanosheet arrays were evenly and orderly distributed on the conductive substrate, its open exposed surface leads the whole electrode materials with excellent rapid charge-discharge performance. When used as a self-supporting electrode, the unique structure displayed an outstanding electrochemical capacitance of $1727.0\mathrm{F}\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ $(1349.2\mathrm{F}\mathbf{g}^{-1})$ at $1\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ . In addition, a hybrid supercapacitor assembled with $\mathrm{CoZnNiS/CNTs/rGO}$ nanoarrays and carbon spheres/rGO achieved an ultra-high volumetric/gravimetric energy density of 65.2 Wh $\mathrm{L}^{-1}/60.4\mathrm{Wh}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ at a power density of 1308 W $\mathrm{L}^{-1}/1200$ W $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ , respectively. Besides, the device also exhibited a satisfactory cycling stability of $90.6\%$ capacitance retention over 10,000 cycles. Furthermore, it illustrated that its wide application prospect in high volumetric energy density supercapacitors.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials
All chemicals used in the experiments were not further purification. Among which, $\mathrm{Co}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ , $Z n(N O_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}.$ - O, 2-methylimidazole, glucose, and ${\mathsf{N a}}_{2}{\mathsf{S}}{\cdot}{\mathsf{S}}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}$ are purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. Carbon nanotubes dispersion $(0.15~\mathrm{wt\%})$ was got from XFNANO Company Limited.
## 2.2. Synthesis
## 2.2.1. Preparation of CoZn-MOF/carbon nanotubes (CNTs)/graphene oxide (GO) composite film
GO nanosheets dispersion with concentration of $0.2~\mathrm{mg/mL}$ are prepared according to the previously reported modified Hummer’s method [28]. Firstly, $100~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of carbon nanotubes dispersion was added into $50~\mathrm{mL}$ of as-prepared GO dispersion and dispersed by ultrasound for 20 min. CNTs/GO film is obtained by vacuum filtrating above mixture and dried at room temperature. Secondly, $0.59{\mathrm{g}}$ of $C_{0}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , $0.30~\mathrm{~g~}~\mathrm{Zn}$ $(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ and $_{1.3\mathrm{~\scriptsize~g~}}$ of 2- methylimidazole were dissolved in $40\ \mathrm{mL}$ deionized water for ultrasound $15\mathrm{min}$ , respectively. After mixing the two nitrate solutions and stirring for one minute, 2-methylimidazole solution was added into the mixed nitrate solution to obtain CoZn-MOF. Finally, the as-prepared CNTs/GO substrate film is soaked into the CoZnMOF mixture for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ at room temperature. The CoZn-MOF/CNTs/ GO composite films can be obtained by washing the samples with anhydrous ethanol and deionized water for 3 to 4 times alternately and drying under $60~^{\circ}\mathsf C$ The CoZn-MOF/CNTs/GO thin film was added into $30~\mathrm{mL}$ deionized water containing $0.24\textrm{g N a}_{2}\mathrm{S}{\cdot}9\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ after reacting at $90^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ , the CoZnS/CNTs/rGO (CZG) thin film was obtained after washed alternately by deionized water and anhydrous ethanol. The packing density of CZG electrode is $1.31\ \mathrm{g\cm}^{-3}$ .
## 2.2.2. Synthesis of CoZnNiS@CNTs/rGO thin film
Firstly, $_{0.02\mathrm{g}}$ of ${\sf N i}({\sf N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}2{\sf H}_{2}0$ was dissolved in $30~\mathrm{mL}$ ethanol solution for ultrasonic dispersion $10\ \mathrm{min}$ , and then transferring into a $40\ \mathrm{\mL}$ Teflon-lined autoclave. Secondly, the ${\mathsf{C o Z n-M O F}}/$ CNTs/GO precursor was infiltrated into above solution, after reacting at $90~^{\circ}C$ for $^{6\mathrm{~h~}}$ , taking out the sample and washing it alternately with anhydrous ethanol and deionized water, and finally drying it in a vacuum at $60~^{\circ}C$ to get the CoZnNi-MOF/CNTs/GO (CCG) sample. The final product of CoZnNiS/CNTs/rGO (CSG) thin film was obtained through a hydrothermal vulcanization reaction. $0.24\mathrm{~g~Na_{2}S{\cdot}9H_{2}O}$ was dissolved in $30~\mathrm{mL}$ deionized water, then the CCG thin film was added into the solution and reacted at $90^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize3h}$ . Finally, the sample was washed alternately with deionized water and anhydrous ethanol and vacuum drying at $60~^{\circ}C$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The packing density of CSG electrode is $1.28\ \mathrm{g\cm}^{-3}$ .
## 2.2.3. Synthesis of carbon sphere/rGO (CG) thin film
The carbon spheres were obtained through a traditional carbohydrolysis of glucose. Typically, $_{\textrm{3g}}$ of glucose was dissolved in $30~\mathrm{mL}$ of distilled water, then transferring the transparent liquid into a $40~\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon and keeping it at $160^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{6\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The obtained dark brown solution is carbon spheres suspension, and its concentration is about $0.1~\mathrm{mg/mL}$ . Then $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of GO suspension and $4~\mathrm{mL}$ of carbon sphere dispersion were mixed together and filtered on a polycarbonate membrane. Finally, the CG film was further annealed at $600^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ in a ${\sf N}_{2}$ flow to improve its conductivity. The packing density of CG electrode is $0.87\ \mathrm{g\cm}^{-3}$ .
## 2.3. Characterization
Composition and crystal phase of composite film were performed by the X-ray diffraction (XRD), using D/MAX-2500 instrument (Rigaku, Japan) with Cu $\mathsf{K}\mathsf{\mathfrak{a}}$ radiation. The surface and section morphology of composite films were observed by a field emission SEM (FESEM, Hitachi Japan S-4800). Further characterization of sample morphology and structure of the composites were tested using transmission electron microscopy (TEM) and highresolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) on a F20 S-TWIN electron microscope at $200~\mathrm{kV}$ acceleration voltage. Further evidence such as composition and chemical state of element of the film are analyzed by the X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS), which is recorded on a Thermo ESCALAB_250Xi with Al $\mathsf{K}\mathsf{\mathfrak{a}}$ radiation sources.
## 2.4. Electrochemical measurements
CHI 760E (Chenhua Shanghai) electrochemical workstation was used throughout the electrochemical performance tests. In threeelectrode system, the obtained binder-free films (CZG, CSG and CG) were directly used as working electrode and sandwiched into two pieces foam nickel sheets, Pt foil was used as a counter electrode, $\mathrm{Hg/HgO}$ electrode was used as a reference electrode, $6\mathrm{~M~}$ KOH solution was used as electrolyte. Electrochemical performance of these films was evaluated via cyclic voltammetry (CV), galvanostatic charge-discharge (GCD) and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) tests.
In two-electrode system, using CSG film as positive electrode and CG film as negative electrode to assemble hybrid supercapacitor. The CV and GCD curves were used to calculate the volumetric/gravimetric specific capacitance and energy density/power density of the device. The calculation results are based on the total volume/mass of the both electrodes.
## 3. Results and discussion
Fig. 1 depicted the preparation procedure of the CoZnNiS/CNTs/ rGO composite film in detail. The CNTs/GO conductive substrate is firstly fabricated through a vacuum filtration method. Then, CoZnMOFs were grown on its surface by a facile solution immersion. Finally, CoZnNiS/CNTs/rGO electrode is obtained by an ion exchanging method and a following hydrothermal vulcanization process.
The morphology of the composite film was firstly observed by SEM. Fig. 2a reveals the cross-section image of CoZn-MOF/CNTs/ GO, we can observe that the 2D structure with triangular shape of CoZn-MOF nanosheets shows smooth surface are arranged on the CNTs/GO substrate. The thickness of the film is about $6~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ . Besides, it can be seen from the inset in Fig. 2a that the CoZnMOF nanosheets and CNTs/GO substrate are closely combined, and such close contact is conducive to the rapid transfer of electrons between different components. The top-view SEM image of CoZn-MOF further confirms that the 2D nanoarray with uniform size are arranged orderly on the surface of CNTs/GO substrate. Fig. 2c and d are the cross-section and top-view SEM images of CoZnNiS/CNTs/rGO. After a series of reactions, the overall morphology of the film is basically maintained, but the surface of the nanosheets becomes rough and thicker. This is due to the replacement of some Zn and Co ions by Ni ions during the ion exchange process, and some O is replaced by S in the process of vulcanization, resulting in the change of morphology. Such unique surface is more conducive to exposing more micro surfaces and providing more active sites for the electrochemical reaction. The inset in Fig. 2d is the picture of the whole film, which can be cut into certain shape and directly used as self-supporting electrode. The microstructure of the CoZnNiS nanosheet was further analysed by TEM and HRTEM. As shown in Fig. 2e, some deep colour wrinkles can be observed on the surface of the nanosheets, which is consistent with the results of SEM. The composition and structure of the CoZnNiS nanosheets were further characterized by HRTEM, as described in Fig. 2f. The interplanar distances of 0.32, 0.29 and $0.30\ \mathrm{nm}$ match well with the (111), (110) and (311) planes of $Z\mathrm{n}_{0.76}C0_{0.24}S$ , $\Nu\dot{\bf1}_{3}\cal S_{2}$ and $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{Co}_{4}\mathrm{S}_{3}}$ phase, respectively [29–31]. It can be concluded that CoZnNiS is composed of three metal sulphides, the crystal structure of the product was characterized by XRD in Fig. 3a. Besides, the corresponding EDS element mappings of the CoZnNiS nanosheet are shown in Fig. 2h–k, indicating the homogenous distribution of the Zn, Co, Ni and S on the whole nanosheet.
XRD (Fig. 3a) was used to further characterize the crystal structure and component of the composites. Compared with the standard card, the main peaks of CoZnNiS at $28.6^{\circ}$ , $47.6^{\circ}$ and $56.5^{\circ}$ match well with (111), (220) and (311) planes of $Z\mathrm{n}_{0.76}\mathrm{C}0_{0.24}\mathrm{S}$ (JCPDS No.47-1656), which is consistent with previous reports [29]. Besides, the relatively weak XRD peaks of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ (JCPDS No.30-0458) and $\Nu\dot{\bf1}_{3}\cal S_{2}$ (JCPDS No.44-1418) can also be observed [30,31], indicating that the obtained product is a complex composed of $Z\mathrm{n}_{0.76}C0_{0.24}S$ , $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ and $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{S}_{2}$ . To further confirm the intrinsic chemical states, elemental composition, and variation in the electronic structure of the CoZnNiS, XPS test is necessary and analysed in detail. Fig. 3b is the XPS full spectrum of CoZnNiS/ CNTs/rGO. It can be seen from the figure that it contains Co, Zn, Ni, S, C and O elements. Fig. 3c shows the high-resolution spectrum of Co and corresponding fitted curves. There are two spin-orbit doublets of Co $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and Co $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ at $779.4\mathrm{eV}$ and $795.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ respectively, confirming the co-existence of Co (III) and Co (II) [32]. The high-resolution spectrum of $\mathsf{N i}\ 2\mathsf{p}$ is shown in Fig. 3d. It can be seen from the fitted graph that the peaks at 854.6 eV and $873.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ correspond to $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ , while the peaks at $855.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $874.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ correspond to $\mathsf{N i}^{3+}$ [33,34]. The high-resolution spectrum of $\mathtt{Z n2p}$ is shown in Fig. 3e. Two strong characteristic peaks at
1021.3 eV and $1044.6\ \mathrm{eV}$ correspond to $Z_{\mathrm{{I}}}\mathrm{{n}}\ 2\mathrm{{p}}_{3/2}$ and $Z_{\mathrm{{n}}}~{2\mathrm{{p}}_{1/2}}$ , respectively [35]. Fig. 3f is the high-resolution spectrum of S 2p. A characteristic peak is formed by $163.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $164.6\ \mathrm{eV}$ (corresponding to $\$2p_{3/2}$ and $\mathsf{S}2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}\mathrm{.}$ ), which could possibly be assigned to metal sulfide and $S^{4+}$ species in sulfate groups, respectively [36]. $\textsf{C1s}$ spectrum is shown in Fig. S1. The peak can be split into three peaks, they are $C-C=C$ (284.3 eV), CAO (285.3 eV) and $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ (287.9 eV) [32].
The electrochemical performance of CSG and CZG are determined using a three-electrode system in a voltage range of $0-$ $0.7~\mathrm{V}.$ as depicted in Fig. 4a and b. Obviously, the area and discharge time of ternary metal sulfide (CSG) are larger than that of binary metal sulfide (CZG), showing its higher electrochemical capacitance. Fig. S2 a and b present the CV curves of CSG and CZG under different scan rates from 2 to $100\mathrm{\mV\}s^{-1}$ . Obviously, at small scan rates, redox peaks can be observed in the two materials, which indicate that redox reaction occurs during charging and discharging process. The possible redox reaction of the sulfides in the alkaline electrolyte can be reasonably elaborated as follows [29,34]:
$$
\begin{array}{l}{{{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}S_{4}\ +\ {\mathsf{O H}}^{-}\ \leftrightarrow\ {\mathsf{C o}}_{3}S_{4}{\mathsf{O H}}\ +\ \mathrm{e}^{-}}}\\ {~}\\ {{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}S_{4}{\mathsf{O H}}\ +\ {\mathsf{O H}}^{-}\ \leftrightarrow\ {\mathsf{C o}}_{3}S_{4}{\mathsf{O}}\ +\ {\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}\ +\ \mathrm{e}^{-}}\\ {~}\\ {{\mathsf{Z n S}}+\ {\mathsf{O H}}^{-}\ \leftrightarrow{\mathsf{Z n S O H}}\ +\ \mathrm{e}^{-}}\\ {~}\\ {{\mathsf{N i}}_{3}S_{2}\ +\ 3{\mathsf{O H}}^{-}\ \leftrightarrow\ {\mathsf{N i}}_{3}S_{2}({\mathsf{O H}})_{3}\ +\ 3\mathrm{e}^{-}}\\ {~}\\ {{\mathsf{N i S}}+\ {\mathsf{O H}}^{-}\ \leftrightarrow\ {\mathsf{N i S O H}}\ +\ \mathrm{e}^{-}}\end{array}
$$
Fig. S2 c and d are the GCD curves of CSG and CZG under different current densities from 1 to $10\mathrm{A}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . Fig. 4c shows more intuitively capacitance values of two materials under different current densities, the volumetric/gravimetric capacitances of CSG film are $1726.7\mathrm{~F~cm}^{-3}$ , $1574.4\ensuremath{\mathrm{~F~}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{cm}}^{-3}$ , $1470.7\mathrm{~F~cm}^{-3}$ , $1391.4\mathrm{~F~}\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ , 1167.4 F $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ and $972.8~\mathrm{~F~}~\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ at the current densities of $1\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ , $2\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , $3\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , $5\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , $8\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ and $10\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ , higher than that of CZG film of $927.5\ \mathrm{F}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ , $880.3\ensuremath{\mathrm{~F~}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{cm}}^{-3}$ , $847.3\ensuremath{\mathrm{~F~}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{cm}}^{-3}$ , $793.9\ \mathrm{F}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ , $718.9\ \mathrm{F}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ and $641.5\ensuremath{\mathrm{~F~}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{cm}}^{-3}$ at the same current densities. The high volumetric capacitance of the proposed selfsupporting CSG film electrode may be mainly ascribed to the following three reasons. Firstly, CoZnNiS is a complex of three kinds of metal sulfides, the synergistic effect between metal sulfides can effectively obtain higher capacitance, besides, CoZnNiS nanosheets with rough surface are more conducive to exposing more micro surfaces and supplying more active sites in the electrochemical reaction. Secondly, In the whole electrode material, $\mathsf{C N T s}/$ rGO film as the conductive substrate is closely combined with CoZnNiS nanosheets, exhibiting excellent conductivity, which is proved in later EIS tests. Thirdly, in general, pursuing better porosity of the electrodes always lead to a very low packing density. In our electrode materials, after loading the CoZnNiS nanosheet arrays, the packing density can reach up to $1.28\ \mathrm{g\cm}^{-3}$ . Therefore, in order to simultaneously obtain volumetric capacitance in an electrode, it is necessary to consider the loaded electrode material with high capacity and optimum the relationship between pore volume and packing density. EIS impedance test is used to further understand the changes in electrochemical performance. Fig. S3 shows the Nyquist impedance curve, comparing the corresponding impedance of CSG and CZG electrodes in the frequency range of ${10}^{6}$ Hz to $0.01~\mathrm{Hz}$ . During the test, no semicircle caused by contact resistance was found in both electrodes. This is because the film was directly used as electrode without excess additives, and there are a lot of pores in the nano wafer that make the electrolyte charge transfer easy, so the contact resistance is very small [37]. In the low frequency Warburg region (related to the ion diffusion kinetics), CSG electrode shows a more vertical straight line than CZG electrodes, showing that the CSG porous structure is more accessible for electrolyte dispersion and ion diffusion [38]. To obtain more information of the energy storage mechanism of the CSG electrode, we further investigate the capacitive contribution $(\mathsf{h}_{1}\nu)$ and the diffusion-controlled contribution $(\mathsf{h}_{2}\nu^{1/2})$ based on the Dunn’s method. The current (i) obeys the following equation: [39]
$$
\mathbf{i}=\mathbf{av}^{\mathrm{b}}
$$
$$
\dot{\mathbf{l}}=\mathsf{h}_{1\mathrm{v}}+\mathsf{h}_{2\mathrm{v}}^{1/2}
$$
where i represents the current response, v is the scan rate, a, b, $\mathbf{h}_{1}$ and $\ensuremath{\mathrm{h}}_{2}$ are computational constants. As shown in Fig. 4d, a good linear relationship can be observed between the peak current and the scan rate with $\mathbf{\bar{R}}^{2}=0.95592$ and 0.91093 for the charging/discharging curves, implying a dominating surface reaction whereas the diffusion-limited bulk process is restricted [38]. Fig. 4f shows the capacitive contribution to the current response, at the scan rates of 2, 5, 10, 20 and $50\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ , the calculated capacitive contributions proportions in the total capacities $20.2\%$ , $24.5\%,$ $29.1\%$ , $36.4\%$ and $57.7\%$ , respectively. Obviously, the proportion of capacitive contribution increases with the increase of scanning rate, implying the capacity of surface oxidation-reduction reaction is dominant at high scanning rate, this may be due to the shortening of ion diffusion and electron transmission distance, similar results have also been reported in related studies. [21,39]
CG film was further prepared and used as the negative electrode. Fig. 5a and b show the cross-section SEM images of CG film, we can observe the thickness of the film is about $3~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ . From the enlarged SEM image in Fig. 5b, it can be seen that the size of carbon spheres is about $100\ \mathrm{nm}$ , and it is evenly inserted into the graphene nanosheets, which is conducive to better diffusion of electrolyte in the process of electrochemical reaction. We also tested the electrochemical properties of the CG film in three-electrode system. Fig. 5c–f show the CV and GCD curves and the capacitance results of the CG film electrode under the voltage range of $-1{-}0\mathrm{V},$ the maximum capacitances can reach up to 145.6 and $178.1\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ at $2{\mathrm{~mV~}}s^{-1}$ and $1\mathrm{A}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively.
The assembly diagram of hybrid supercapacitor is briefly described in Fig. 6a. CSG film and CG film were directly used as positive and negative electrode, respectively. Firstly, we test the CV curves of the two materials in a three-electrode system to determine the possible voltage window of the device. As shown in Fig. 6b, the voltage window of the positive is $_{0-0.7\mathrm{V}}$ , and the negative is $-1{-}0\vee$ , therefore, the voltage window of the hybrid may extend to $1.7{\mathrm{V}}.$ In order to further confirm the voltage window of the device, we tested the CV curves in the two-electrode system (Fig. 6c), and it can be seen that polarization occurs when the voltage exceeds $1.6\mathrm{V}$ , which shows that the suitable voltage window of the device is $_{0-1.6}\lor.$ . Fig. 6d and e are the CV and GCD curves the device under the potential window of $_{0-1.6\mathrm{~V~}}$ . Obviously, there is no obvious changes in the shape of the CV curves and GCD curves, indicating the excellent rate performance of the hybrid supercapacitor. In Fig. 6e, we can observe that the discharge time is shorter than the charge time, the charge discharge time is determined by the loop resistance. It is due to the high loop resistance during charging and small loop resistance during discharge, resulting in shorter discharge time than charging time. Fig. 6f shows the capacitance of the device at different current densities. The capacitances are $185.1\ \mathrm{~F~}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ , $171.2\ensuremath{\mathrm{~F~}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{cm}}^{-3}$ , $162.8\ \mathrm{~F~}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ , $148.1\ \mathrm{F}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ , $129.1~\ensuremath{\mathrm{~F~}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{cm}}^{-3}$ and $117.2~\mathrm{~F~}~\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ at the current densities of $1.5\mathsf{A g}^{-1}$ , $2\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , $3\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , $5\mathsf{A g}^{-1}$ , $8\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ and $10\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. Moreover, the current density becomes 6.7 times of the original, and the capacity retention still maintain $63.3\%$ , also suggesting the good rate property of the device.
One of the important properties of the device is cyclic stability, as shown in Fig. 7a, after 10,000 cycles, the value can still maintain $90.6\%$ of the original capacitance, which shows that the supercapacitor has good application value. We also characterize the morphology of the sample after the cyclic performance. The surface of the film has certain fracture, which may be caused by the pressure when assembling the supercapacitor, but the structure of the nanosheets still remained, indicating the electrode has good stability. We further calculate the gravimetric and volumetric energy density of hybrid supercapacitor in the Fig. 7b and c. Our device exhibited a maximum gravimetric energy density of $60.4\ W\mathrm{h}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ at $1200~\mathrm{W~kg}^{-1}$ , which is comparable with previously reported
MOFs-based supercapacitor, NiO/C///Porous carbon $(35.9\mathsf{W h}\mathsf{k g}^{-1})$ 1 [40], NiCo-S//AC (22.6 W h $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ ) [41], GM-LEG $@$ Ni-MOF//AC (44.0 W $\mathbf{h\kg^{-1}}$ ) [42], ADC-OH/rGO//ADC-C/rGO (59.0 W h $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ ) [43], Cu-MOF/G//AC (34.5 W h $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ ) [44] and ${\mathsf{N i C o-M O F/r G O}}//$ rGO (W h $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ ) [45]. It is also gratifying that its volumetric energy density can reach up to 65.2 W $\mathrm{~h~L~}^{-1}$ at $1308\mathrm{W}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ . This value is larger than most of carbon-based supercapacitor devices, such as GFNS/AC (49.66 W h ${\mathrm{L}}^{-1}$ ) [7], Ultra-thick graphene//Ultra-thick graphene ( $64.7\mathrm{~W~h~L^{-1}}.$ [46], $\mathrm{NaxMnO_{2}/C C//M o O_{2}/C C}$ (1.6 W h ${\sf L}^{-1}$ ) [47], $\mathrm{GH@NC@Co_{9}S_{8}//G H@N C(}$ (28.7 W h ${\mathrm{L}}^{-1}$ ) [16], GNC/ $\mathsf{M n O}_{2}//\mathsf{G N C}$ (45.9 W h ${\sf L}^{-1}$ ) [48] and $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}@\mathrm{TiN}~\ @\mathrm{CNT})//\mathrm{C}\ @\mathrm{TiN}$ $\mathsf{N W s@C N T}(61.2\mathsf{W h}\mathsf{L}^{-1})$ [49]. In order to further prove the supercapacitor’s application in practical, 7 blue and 6 white LEDs can be lighted by two devices in series as shown in Fig. 7d. In our research system, the MOFs/CNTs/GO composite precursor can be used to prepare various MOFs derivative composite films for electrode in other energy storage system.
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, the MOFs-derived ZnCoNiS nanosheet arrays on CNTs/rGO substrate were successfully constructed. Beneficial from its large exposed surface areas and outstanding electrical conductivity, the as-prepared electrode showed high volumetric/gravimetric capacitance and rate capability. More importantly, the hybrid supercapacitor was assembled with CoZnNiS/CNTs/rGO film as the positive electrode and carbon spheres/rGO the negative electrode exhibits high volumetric energy density as well as excellent cyclic stability. Therefore, the concept of using CNTs/rGO as a conductive substrate and two-dimensional MOFs as a sacrificial agent could be extended to the preparation of different types of other two-dimensional multi metal compounds for high volumetric capacitance in microelectronic device.
